  "content": "# Travel Journal: The Ascent to Kharbu\n\n*October 14th \u2014 Day Three*\n\nThe road ended where the map did, which is to say it didn't really end so much as it gave up. I left the jeep at dawn, when the village of Kharbu still wore its blanket of mist, and began the final climb on foot along a path that the locals had worn smooth over centuries of goats and pilgrims. My pack weighed nearly as much as the air was thin, and by the second hour my lungs burned with a sharpness I had forgotten mountains could produce. The weather turned on me around mid-morning, as it seemed determined to do here. One moment the sun broke through in a shaft of gold that set the snowfields blazing; the next, grey clouds rolled off the peaks like cotton pulled across glass, and a cold drizzle turned the path to slick mud. I pulled my rain shell tight and kept moving, because I had learned by now that the mountain does not negotiate.\n\nBy noon I reached the first cluster of stone houses, their flat roofs stacked with drying hay and their windows small against the cold. An old woman sat outside her door, her face a map of deeper wrinkles than I thought possible,",
